#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/daily-bench <version> [--soak 8h] [--compare <version>] [--suite <name> ...] [--runs N]
tools/daily-bench <version> --report [--compare <version>]

Tacit Daily: whether Tacit is fit to use every day, measured the same way on every release in the test VM on the cloud
machine (cef-build-x64), through its guest agent (tools/vm run), as tools/vm-smoke is. The VM must be running a disk
with <version> installed, booted with this checkout's tools/vm (vm disk). It only reports for now (the owner,
2026-11-12): after 2-3 releases have set the normal spread it joins the release gate, with margins.

  the short run (every release; 25-30 minutes with --runs 3, a third of it the 10 minutes at rest): each daily task
      --runs times, the speed of starting, searching and loading, the memory, processor and wakeups at rest, the space
      caches take, and what went wrong on the way
  --soak 8h (before each weekly release): the tasks over and over for that long, to catch leaks and slow decay, then
      an update from a test repo through Settings' Restart
  --suite creator|office|developer|gamer|everyday|all: the workflow suites too, one per kind of person who uses Tacit
      (round2/BRIEF-DAILY-BENCH-WORKFLOWS.md); apps they need are got through Apps on the VM
  --lang de|ru: the run in another language and keyboard, set as Setup sets them (German: de_DE.UTF-8, de; Russian:
      ru_RU.UTF-8, us,ru switched with Alt+Shift), with the checks that only fail there: Settings' Network page shows
      an IP, a VPN shows as connected (a WireGuard stand-in made in the VM), the lock takes the password typed with that
      layout active; the computer is put back to its own language at the end
  --lived-in [--home <file>]: the VM on the release before <version>, given a lived-in home (that release's settings
      file with its old defaults, a copied hypridle.conf, the old kb_layout override, pins, a wallpaper, a mirror list
      of its own), updated through Restart from tools/vm-smoke's test repo, then judged on that home: what an update
      must get right on a computer in use, then the short run; <file> from the shell's tests/config/releases
      (lived.json, a file that went through every release; TACIT_SHELL_RELEASES says where they are)
  --suites-only: the suites without the short run's tasks and measures (with --lang, its checks still run)
  --repeat N: the same steps N times on the same build, each from the known state: each step's agreement (3 of 3 the
      same, or unstable); an unstable step is marked so and left out of the comparison until it is the same every time
  --record <dir>: every exchange with the VM saved in <dir> (exchanges.jsonl, with the run's record and scorecard), for
      tools/daily-bench-test to replay: a real run's answers become the self-test's
  --report: the scorecard written again from the record, the VM untouched

Every part (the language checks, the app starts, the tasks, the time at rest, each suite, the starts from power on)
begins from the same saved state of the computer (vm snapshot load daily-base), and the run ends by loading the
computer as it found it (daily-start). Each check comes out worked, failed, or could not be measured: the VM's
machinery not answering (tried once more first), a mirror or a site, or the step's own setup; that is never a pass and
never Tacit's failure.

A failure that is a mirror's, a site's or the network's (an app Apps could not download, YouTube's own changes, a
check for updates whose mirror did not answer) is reported apart, as outside Tacit, and does not count against it.

The test account's password (for the sign-in, the lock, a sleep's wake and a restart) is read from TACIT_VM_PASSWORD
here, or ~/tacit-vm/password on the cloud machine; without it those parts are not run, and the scorecard says so.
TACIT_VM_KEYS=de|gb when the VM types German or British.

Written to out/x86_64/: daily-<version>.json (every number and check), daily-<version>.md (the scorecard, with the
comparison against the release before); a second run of the same version is daily-<version>.2.*, and pairs of runs
of one version measure the spread the margins start from. --soak writes daily-<version>-soak.*, --lived-in and --lang
add -lived-in and -<lang>; each is compared only with runs of its own kind.
"""
